**Source review findings.** Solo and Versus can discard a fresh action/pickup pulse on the mandatory neutral Resume tick. In Solo, an ordinary two-tick desktop callback can conceal this loss; a one-tick high-refresh callback or earlier zero-tick poll exposes it. Versus Resume and Team fresh Start/Retry can also inherit a prior menu interval. The repair retains fresh pulses in existing input owners, discards them on Pause/recovery/capture-stop, resets accepted entry clocks and preserves 120 Hz simulation, replay formats and the strictly-greater-than-250 ms interruption rule. Existing held-controller blocking remains. The new controls cover 120/144 Hz, zero-tick polling, catch-up, admitted short controller presses, visible touch controls and stale menu gaps. They remain unexecuted. These defects were found during review; their introduction by #1544 and contribution to the reported device hitches are not established.

A cold official/bundled soundtrack is synchronously inspected in `soundtrack-source.mjs` before `soundtrack-player.mjs` reaches its worker; next-track preparation can take this path during active play. This is a confirmed reachable cost, not proof it caused the reported hitches. The repair uses the existing transport inspector and privately reuses successful facts only for the exact same immutable Blob within that one load. Both pinned-integrity comparisons remain, and other readers still receive normal validation. The [File API](https://w3c.github.io/FileAPI/#dfn-Blob) defines Blob bytes as immutable; this does not justify trusting arbitrary caller metadata.

A bounded cross-PR audio review of #1694, #1619 and #1675 found no additional compatibility defect. Solo and Couch preserve the delegated inspection options, current-plus-next limits and streamed playback. Explicit gesture activation remains independent of the editor import. If the worker is unavailable, fallback stages remain deferred while playing and the existing sixty-second acquisition deadline may expire; this needs fault-injection and native transition qualification, not a synchronous gameplay fallback.

The wider audio audit found bounded but unscheduled cold recorded-speech parsing during play. Current misses immediately show captions and only prepare a future occurrence; warmup is opportunistic and Start does not await it. At the existing two MiB input cap, a valid WAV can still contain 262,140 chunks. Keep cold-demand speech profiling explicit before changing cue readiness or adding another worker. Demo's explicit Play Style action can prepare a complete saved MP3 library while Demo runs; profile this separately from Main's current/next transport. Other inspected Main MP3 scans are explicit paused Studio/import/export or separately owned offline work.

A bounded review of main's First Flight Home change found no missing required control or stale FPV shell in the inspected owners. The main page supplies the new button, compiled editions use the canonical page, and the FPV generator excludes those three changed production inputs. This is source compatibility evidence; native Home layout and interaction remain unqualified.

A joint source review of lifecycle #1693 and artwork #1695 found their app changes disjoint: artwork changes acquisition/representation, while lifecycle changes fixed-step input retirement. Required picture verification/decoding still precedes the accepted Start/Resume clock and input reset. This is static compatibility evidence; combined execution remains required.

A separate bounded source review found no additional concrete defect in cadence freezing, cheap stable 30 Hz treatment, immediate damage/capture HUD ordering, or cell/terrain/picture/resize cache invalidation. The numeric HUD test alone does not prove sub-100 ms delivery; that conclusion follows from source ordering. Native raster, orientation and sustained adaptation checks remain open.

Targeted Canvas2D review found display/scratch ownership, paused restoration, cache rebuilding and explicit Resume controls already present, with source tests for multiple lost surfaces and pending-readiness cancellation. Offline observation detaches independently; only explicit owned cancellation requests worker pause. No additional renderer/offline change was justified by this review, and native recovery still needs qualification.

The save/export audit preserves Solo's intentional readable display versus compact download and its existing recorded-actor/raw ownership behavior. A proposed shared terminal optimization was not implemented because that helper serves Team/Versus and would not close Solo's long-recording item. Paused Solo backup conversion and Team/Versus duplicate serialization remain P2 profiling candidates. Proof validation and durable-save guarantees stay intact.

